        &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Flag request failed: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;FlagReply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setEnabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;failures&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;failures&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;failures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;240&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;enabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;New route planning panel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The default is off. A failed poll does not expose a beta control, and repeated failures back off to four minutes. The backend separately honors an upstream &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; by passing it through; a production client can parse that header and schedule the next attempt accordingly. Keep the business event richer than this UI state: record the flag key, evaluated value, region, release identifier, and event time in your own instrumentation when the dispatch action occurs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not treat polling as monitoring. There are no alert or notification routes for thresholds, phone, SMS, or webhooks, and there is no synthetic or heartbeat monitoring. A scheduled logistics task that never ran is a different failure mode; use a service such as Healthchecks for that signal. Logs can carry &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;span_id&lt;/code&gt; for correlation, but there is no distributed-trace query or span tree here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When is a specialist the better runner-up?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose LaunchDarkly when built-in flag history and evaluation data are requirements rather than optional evidence. Choose Unleash when an open-source deployment model and its established flag workflow outweigh the convenience of a shared backend API. ConfigCat is another focused hosted choice when its SDK-driven delivery model fits the frontend better than maintaining a proxy and polling loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those products reduce flag-specific glue. The shared API reduces a different kind of glue: the handoff between an application and multiple backend capabilities can use one HTTP surface, one key, and a self-describing contract. That distinction matters. A team already paying the operational cost of a specialist platform should not migrate merely to make one toggle call look tidier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the evidence side, Sentry is the better fit when error grouping and application failures dominate the investigation. Datadog fits teams that need a broad commercial observability suite, while Grafana fits teams assembling dashboards and telemetry around an existing observability stack. None of those three replaces flag evaluation by itself. They are places to correlate the release and business events that make a logistics incident reconstructable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a basic beta panel or staged regional launch, the proxy above is enough. Teams already standardizing several backend capabilities behind one key should try Infrai for this narrow flag boundary because provider swaps do not force application contract changes. For experiments, complex dependencies, real-time updates, or audit-heavy release governance, use a specialist. No amount of wrapper code turns polling into streaming or creates evidence that was never recorded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai API discovery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://launchdarkly.com/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LaunchDarkly documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.getunleash.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unleash documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://configcat.com/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ConfigCat documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://healthchecks.io/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Healthchecks documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5424" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RFC 5424: The Syslog Protocol&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this boundary fits your system, start with the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;platform documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Diseñar un inbox temporal en React no consiste solo en mostrar mensajes: consiste en explicar cada estado, respetar el foco y mantener la interfaz rápida.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  El problema: esperar no es un estado
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En una prueba de registro, una persona puede estar esperando un mensaje de verificación en un inbox temporal. La pantalla suele empezar con un spinner, luego cambia a una lista vacía, después muestra un error o finalmente enseña el correo recibido. Si esos cambios solo se ven como variaciones visuales, el flujo se vuelve confuso para quien usa un lector de pantalla, navega con teclado o tiene una conexión lenta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El error de diseño mas común es tratar &lt;code&gt;loading&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;empty&lt;/code&gt; y &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt; como detalles del componente. En realidad son información de producto. Cada uno debe responder tres preguntas: qué está pasando, qué puede hacer la persona y cuándo volverá a cambiar la pantalla.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Este enfoque es especialmente útil cuando probamos una &lt;em&gt;burner email address&lt;/em&gt;: la bandeja tiene una vida corta, el mensaje puede tardar y repetir la acción sin control puede crear carreras entre solicitudes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Un pequeño modelo de estados
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Antes de escribir JSX, dibujo una máquina sencilla. No hace falta una librería para esto; basta con que el componente tenga una fuente de verdad clara.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;InboxState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;ready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;empty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;checkedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;La ventaja es que el diseño no depende de una combinación imposible de booleanos como &lt;code&gt;isLoading&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;hasError&lt;/code&gt; y &lt;code&gt;hasMessages&lt;/code&gt;. Un estado excluye a los otros. Para una bandeja de correo de prueba, también añadiría un identificador de ejecución y una regla de caducidad: así una respuesta vieja no pisa el resultado de la sesión actual.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En el estado &lt;code&gt;empty&lt;/code&gt;, conviene decir que la consulta terminó y no encontró mensajes todavía. “Sin mensajes” es distinto de “no hemos podido consultar”. Ese matiz ahorra varios reintentos innecesarios y hace el flujo mas honesto.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Feedback accesible sin mover el foco
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La llegada de un mensaje no debería enviar el foco al principio de la página. Si alguien está revisando el formulario o usando el botón de actualizar con teclado, ese salto rompe su contexto. Mantengo el foco donde estaba y anuncio solo el cambio importante mediante una región viva.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;InboxStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;InboxState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Buscando mensajes…&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;ready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; mensaje(s) disponible(s)`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;empty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;No hay mensajes nuevos todavía&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`No se pudo actualizar: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-atomic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;role="status"&lt;/code&gt; permite comunicar una actualización sin interrumpir de forma agresiva. Los controles visibles siguen siendo necesarios: una región viva no reemplaza un botón “Actualizar” con nombre accesible, un estado de foco perceptible y mensajes de error asociados al control que puede resolverlos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para una interfaz mas completa, el botón puede quedar deshabilitado mientras se consulta, pero no debería desaparecer. También muestro una hora de última comprobación en texto normal. Es un detalle pequeño, aunque ayuda a distinguir un inbox vacío de una pantalla que dejó de responder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si el usuario pulsa actualizar, cancelo la solicitud anterior o ignoro su resultado con un &lt;code&gt;requestId&lt;/code&gt;. Esta protección complementa una &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/react-valida-email-sin-respuestas-viejas-33od"&gt;validación de email sin respuestas viejas&lt;/a&gt;: el principio es el mismo, cada resultado debe pertenecer a la acción que sigue vigente.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Rendimiento: actualizar lo necesario
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un inbox temporal no necesita volver a renderizar toda la aplicación cada pocos segundos. Mantengo la consulta y el estado cerca de la pantalla que los usa, memoizo filas realmente costosas y evito crear estructuras nuevas en cada render si no aportan valor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También separo la frecuencia de consulta de la animación. Un indicador CSS sutil consume menos atención que un componente que cambia continuamente el árbol de accesibilidad. Cuando llega un mensaje, resalto la fila una sola vez y dejo que la persona decida si quiere abrirla.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El layout debe reservar espacio para el estado de carga y para los mensajes de error. Este patrón reduce saltos visuales y conecta bien con estas notas sobre &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/css-para-errores-de-email-sin-cls-4h2e"&gt;errores de email sin CLS&lt;/a&gt;. No hace falta una transición espectacular; una interfaz estable se siente mas rápida porque conserva las referencias visuales.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si el producto usa &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tempmailso&lt;/a&gt; como fuente de una dirección temporal para pruebas, lo trato como una dependencia reemplazable. El componente recibe una función &lt;code&gt;getMessages&lt;/code&gt; y no conoce los detalles de red. Así los tests pueden devolver &lt;code&gt;empty&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt; o &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt; de forma determinista, sin depender de que llegue un correo real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist para revisar el componente
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Antes de darlo por terminado, reviso estos puntos:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Cada solicitud tiene un estado visible y un texto comprensible?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La llegada de un mensaje se anuncia sin robar el foco?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Los botones tienen nombre, foco visible y un tamaño cómodo?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El estado vacío explica qué hacer a continuación?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Una respuesta vieja puede sobrescribir la sesión actual?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El layout mantiene su espacio cuando aparece un error?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Puedo probar &lt;code&gt;loading&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;empty&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt; y &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt; sin red?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El texto “dummy e mail” puede aparecer en un caso de prueba o en una nota de QA, pero no debería convertirse en el nombre de un control ni en el mensaje principal de la interfaz. La terminología visible debe ayudar a entender el producto, no repetir una búsqueda poco clara.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La idea final es sencilla: un inbox temporal es un pequeño sistema de estados con una interfaz humana encima. Cuando React expresa esos estados con claridad, Accessibility deja de ser una capa añadida al final. Se convierte en parte del diseño, y el rendimiento pasa a medirse por algo muy concreto: cuánto contexto conserva la persona mientras espera.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've made music as theverychap since 1994. It started on an Amiga A1200 with OctaMED, and the earliest stuff is likely what you'd expect from that: breakbeats, samples galore, and jungle and techno tunes. The bulk of it comes from 1996 to 2001, when I was making music pretty much all day and night. There's a bit of a gap after 2008, and since 2021 I've been at it again (during &lt;em&gt;that&lt;/em&gt; period we all went through).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are over 150 tracks on 20-odd (rough) collections, and for years they sat on my old site as a list. Lists are fine if you already know what you're after, but nobody does with someone else's "back catalogue".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Six ways in
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So... I rebuilt &lt;a href="https://theverychap.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;theverychap.com&lt;/a&gt; around different ways of exploring the same music. The Sonic Atlas places every track by how it sounds, tempo against brightness, energy, or dynamics, and if you turn on Drift, the player follows the closest-sounding track you haven't heard yet. The Timeline lays everything out in the order it was made, each strip drawn from the track's own waveform. The Tuner is an FM dial where every track is a station, always on air. The Mood mixer gives you faders for tempo, brightness, energy, and era, and plays the closest match. The Crate lets you flip through records, each one previewing its most energetic stretch. And the Gallery is there for anyone who just wants a tracklist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All of that depends on knowing how each track sounds, so every one was analysed for tempo, energy, brightness, and dynamics. Tempo came from Essentia, and where it wasn't confident I checked by ear. The site runs on Laravel, React, and Inertia, with a Filament admin for the releases and tracks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The descriptions came across from the old site as I wrote them at the time. "A pretty rubbish remix of a Justin Robertson tune done for the sake of it one night" survived the move, and so did "socks on tape", a release of tunes originally recorded to tape (like most!). I'll be tagging and labelling them further. It's nice for me to be able to explore what I made back in the day, and hopefully for anyone that's interested also... Maybe it's not the music that excites - maybe it's the beautiful UIs!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've got an archive and structured data of anything, I'd say give it more than one way of being explored. Someone who'd never scroll a tracklist might well turn a radio dial!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most budgeting apps want a subscription. YNAB is around $100 a year, and the free ones often keep the useful parts, like importing your bank statement, behind a paywall. Paying every month for an app whose whole job is helping me spend less never sat right with me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built my own. Budget Ctrl is a desktop budgeting app for Windows and Linux. It's free and MIT licensed: no subscription, no premium tier, no ads. Your budget never leaves your computer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also follows payday rather than the calendar month. You tell it when you're paid, and each budget period runs from one payday to the next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmjcv24yszt7lfctkw6yq.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmjcv24yszt7lfctkw6yq.png" alt=" " width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post is about the parts that turned out harder than they looked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No account, no server, no bank login&lt;br&gt;
The whole budget is one JSON file in your app data folder. The only thing the app connects to is GitHub, to check for updates, and you can turn that off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Part of that is privacy. Plaid, which links bank accounts to a lot of budgeting apps, agreed to pay $58 million in 2021 to settle a class action over how it collected people's bank data (without admitting wrongdoing). The other part is that apps disappear. Mint shut down in 2024, and when Level Money closed it deleted the transaction history of every account people had linked from other banks. A file on your own disk can't be switched off by someone else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is that you import bank statements instead of syncing. Bank sync is the thing people complain about most in other budgeting apps (connections that need logging into again every week or two, transactions missing or doubled after a reconnect), so I'm happy with that trade.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;The stack:&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tauri v2 for the desktop shell, React 19 and Vite for the UI&lt;br&gt;
Tauri's built-in tray icon, plus plugins for notifications, start-at-login, single instance and updates&lt;br&gt;
A Windows installer of about 3.7 MB&lt;br&gt;
377 Vitest tests. The logic lives in plain functions under src/lib, away from React, which makes it easy to test &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Payday periods&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A budget period is a month shifted by a cutoff day. Paid on the 10th, the "September" period runs from 10 September to 9 October. Most things fall out of that easily. Recurring bills don't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A bill due on the 5th belongs to the period it actually lands in, which here is 5 October. A bill on the 31st has to land on the 30th in a 30-day month and on the 28th or 29th in February. This is the function that decides:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Resolves which actual date inside the period a given dayOfMonth lands on.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// With cutoff 10 and period 2026-09: day 15 -&amp;gt; 15 Sep; day 5 -&amp;gt; 5 Oct.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;recurringDateInPeriod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dayOfMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;periodKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cutoffDay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;periodRange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;periodKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cutoffDay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dayOfMonth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tryIn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;daysInCalMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;tryIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getFullYear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;tryIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getFullYear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A bill only counts against what you have left once its day has passed. So the "Remaining" figure is money you still have, not money you'll have spent by the end of the period.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Bank statements&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bank CSV exports have no common format, and Polish banks have their own habits. Many write Windows-1250 instead of UTF-8, separate columns with semicolons, use decimal commas, and put a block of account details above the actual table. So the importer assumes nothing. It works out the encoding, separator, header row, date order and what each column means, and lets you correct its guess.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Parsing turned out to be the easy part. The hard part is counting things twice. A statement contains the rent standing order (already a recurring bill in the budget), the salary (already the period's income) and the shop you typed in by hand on Tuesday. Imported blindly, all of those count twice. So the importer matches every row against what the budget already holds and leaves anything it thinks is already counted unticked. Import rules handle the rest: tell it once that BIEDRONKA means Food, and it remembers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amounts bit me too, in a place I didn't expect. The importer had a proper amount parser, but two forms in the app still used parseFloat(value.replace(",", ".")). Typing "1 234,56" saved 1, and "1.234,56" saved 1.234. That's not great in an app with a Polish translation. Both use the same parser now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Not losing anyone's data&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I spent more time here than anywhere else. A budgeting app that loses a year of entries is worse than no app at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The desktop app keeps the budget in a real file rather than in the webview's localStorage. Webview storage can be cleared along with browser data, while a file is something you can copy and back up yourself. The file is still mirrored to localStorage as a fallback. There's a backup each day, taken before that day's first change, plus one before anything risky: imports, restores, starting over, installing an update. The last 14 are kept.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A code review before the last release found two problems that could still lose data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A damaged file meant an empty budget. If the file was cut short, by a crash in the middle of a save for example, the app couldn't read it and quietly started an empty budget. Your next edit would have saved over the real one. Now the damaged file is set aside, the last good copy is used if there is one, and otherwise a recovery screen lists your backups. Nothing is saved until you pick one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Saves could finish out of order. Some inputs save on every keystroke. Two writes of the whole file in flight at once can finish in either order, and if the older one finishes last, it wins. The fix is small: queue the writes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The queue has a test that starts a slow write and two quick ones, and it fails if you take the queue out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Updates without a server&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pushing a version tag starts a GitHub Actions workflow. It runs the tests, builds the Windows and Linux installers, signs them with the project's update key, and puts them in a draft release together with a latest.json manifest. The app checks that manifest once a day, shows a banner when there's something new, and installs it when you click. Tauri's updater refuses anything not signed with the project's key. Because the release starts as a draft, nothing reaches anyone until I've checked the signatures and downloaded the files myself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One small gotcha: 0.5.1 went out with empty release notes in the app's "What's new". The job that writes latest.json hadn't been given the notes. Now the job that opens the draft passes them along.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing isn't solved yet. The Windows installer isn't code-signed, so SmartScreen warns the first time you install it ("More info", then "Run anyway"). Updates from inside the app don't trigger it. Certificates cost money every year, so I plan to apply to SignPath Foundation, which signs open-source projects for free, once the project has more users behind it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Five Languages&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The app speaks English, Polish, German, Spanish and Chinese. Two things caught me out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plurals. English has two forms. Polish has three: 1 rachunek, 2 rachunki, 5 rachunków, and 22 takes the same form as 2 while 12 takes the same form as 5. Intl.PluralRules knows the rules, so each translation stores its forms in the order its language needs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PLURAL_FORMS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;de&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;one&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;other&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;one&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;other&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;pl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;one&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;few&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;many&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;zh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;other&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PLURAL_FORMS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;indexOf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Intl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PluralRules&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Category names. The built-in categories used to be saved in whatever language you set up in, so switching from Polish back to English left "Jedzenie" everywhere. Now they're stored under English names and translated when shown.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;What's next&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Weekly and fortnightly pay periods, for everyone who isn't paid monthly&lt;br&gt;
MT940 and OFX statement import, alongside CSV&lt;br&gt;
Budgets for households: who paid and who owes whom first, then one budget shared between two computers through a folder you already sync&lt;br&gt;
For 2.0, a phone app, still with no account and no server&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Try it&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Windows installer, an AppImage and a .deb are on the releases page: &lt;a href="https://github.com/CptMeowgi/budgetctrl/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/CptMeowgi/budgetctrl/releases&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you try it, tell me what breaks or what's missing. Issues are open. And if you've built sync between a desktop app and a phone app without a server in the middle, I'd like to hear how you did it.&lt;br&gt;
_&lt;br&gt;
This post was written with help from an AI assistant. I checked the details myself._&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hi everyone! 👋 &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a solo developer and indie hacker, I've always struggled with two big bottlenecks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scattered growth workflows:&lt;/strong&gt; Trying to balance content marketing, lead generation, and social media outreach without burning out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payment friction:&lt;/strong&gt; Setting up heavy payment systems often takes hours, when all you need is a dead-simple, instant micro-checkout solution for lightweight digital tools.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

